<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#new-economic-mechanism">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Economic Mechanism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New Economic Mechanism is the name of market-oriented reforms in two communist states. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, introduced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janos-kadar">János Kádár</a>'s government in 1968, it gave state enterprises more freedom and allowed some market prices, making Hungary the most liberal economy in the Soviet bloc. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a>, from 1986, it opened the economy to private enterprise and foreign investment.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under János Kádár the regime softened, a general amnesty came in 1963 and the <b>New Economic Mechanism</b> of 1968 let market elements into the planned economy, so that Hungary was called the happiest barrack of the Eastern bloc.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After harsh reprisals, Kádár allowed a mixed economy under the <b>New Economic Mechanism</b> of 1968, making Hungary the most liberal state in the Soviet bloc.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-peoples-republic">Hungarian People's Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1986 the <b>New Economic Mechanism</b> opened the economy to markets and foreign investment.</blockquote><a href="/state/lao-peoples-democratic-republic">Lao People's Democratic Republic</a></li></ul></section>
